<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pavlos Kountouriotis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1855">1855</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pavlos Kountouriotis was a Greek admiral and statesman. He commanded the Greek fleet in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>, winning the battles of Elli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>. In 1916 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> and General Danglis in the triumvirate of the Provisional Government of National Defence, and from 1924 to 1929 he was the first president of the Second Hellenic Republic, with a short break in 1926.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pavlos-kountouriotis.jpg" alt="Pavlos Kountouriotis" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vaka, Demetra · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlmiranteCoundouritis--inheartofgermani00vaka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…at Goudi in 1909, the army and navy performed well in the Balkan Wars of 1912–13: Greek forces entered Thessaloniki on 8 November 1912, took Ioannina in 1913, and defeated Bulgaria in the Second Balkan War, while the fleet commanded by <b>Pavlos Kountouriotis</b> won control of the Aegean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In September and October Venizelos formed a provisional government there with Admiral <b>Pavlos Kountouriotis</b> and General Panagiotis Danglis.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-national-defence">Provisional Government of National Defence</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Greece was thus replaced, with Admiral <b>Pavlos Kountouriotis</b> as first president.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-hellenic-republic">Second Hellenic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydra-greece">Ýdra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.34,23.47&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Ýdra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5137 820 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pavlos Kountouriotis"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5587 1090h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5587 1090h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.34,23.47&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
